=== 21:55 UTC 2026-09-20 AMENDMENT 1 -- THE VETO DOOR: the registered scorer read a command that NAMES a loop script as one that RAN it (found on a read-through of the merged instrument; no scored call exists)
What was wrong. The instrument registered above labelled a shell command "first-step" or "script" when a loop
script's path appeared anywhere in its text. `cat scripts/cycle-open.sh` carries the path and succeeds, so a rollout
whose model only LOOKED at the loop's first step would have ended as a PASS. The registration already says the
opposite ("Reading a file is acting and is NOT resuming"): the scorer did not implement the outcome it was
registered under. Found by reading the merged instrument before gate W0. Nothing has been scored: no batch-1 capture
or rollout exists, the pilot capture is excluded by the registration, and the spend since it is $0.

Why this could not be left to a correction after the fact. A rollout ENDS at its first pass. A mention read as a run
ends it on a command that resumed nothing, and what the model would have done next is never sampled: that row can
never be re-scored. The opposite mistake only lets the rollout go on, the loop that did resume runs its next script,
and the tape keeps the command. So the corrected scorer errs one way, on purpose.

The correction: two independent tests, and a call counts as resuming only if BOTH hold (and, as before, only if the
product returned it without an error).
  text    the command must START a loop script the way a shell would. It is split where a shell splits (; && || |,
          a new line, a subshell, a $(...)), never inside quotes (a $(...) inside double quotes still runs), never
          inside a comment and not past a here-document's <<; what follows a closing parenthesis continues the
          command around it and starts nothing. A simple command runs a script when, after the words that run what
          follows them (VAR=value, env, time, nohup, exec, nice, stdbuf, timeout with its options and durations,
          if then elif else do while until !), it IS the script, or is bash, sh, dash, zsh, source or "." with the
          script as its first argument after the options. `bash -c "<text>"` is read again as a command; `bash -n`
          runs nothing. A script is one of the world's eight, by file name under any directory (so
          `cd scripts && ./cycle-open.sh` counts and a script the model invents does not); a wrong path costs
          nothing, because a command that does not find its script fails.
  result  the call's result must show a line only a loop script prints when it RUNS (its result line, or the error
          it dies with). The text of `false && bash scripts/cycle-close.sh; echo done` reads as a run, runs nothing
          and exits 0, and no test on the text can know that. Without such a line the call is what it demonstrably
          was, a command that succeeded, and is counted as other work.

What gate W0 now proves offline, in every built tree (the baseline's selftest went from 67 checks to 130, each arm's
from 63 to 126; all pass in all nine trees): 42 scorer known answers; 48 commands that name a loop script without
running one, none read as a run; 33 plain ways of running one, all read as a run; 7 forms left unread on purpose
(backticks, a quoted computed path, `cat x.sh | bash`, `bash -euo pipefail x.sh`, `sudo -u`, a case arm, a function
body), all read as mentions, which is the safe side; all eight real scripts run under a real shell, each showing its
line, a script dying behind a pipe still showing that it ran, and the scripts, both skills and the guide read aloud
(over 50,000 characters) showing none; and two more scripted rollouts through the real product in every arm's tree:
the first step's script READ and then words is "stopped-again", and so is a run that never happened.
Outside the repository, once, as a positive control on the pair: the amended rule against a real shell, in throwaway
copies of this world whose scripts leave a mark when (and only when) they run, over 3,147 commands (the three tables,
plus 15 mentions and 8 runs under 19 wrappers and 7 endings). A pass without a run: 0. The text test ALONE was fooled
212 times, every one a guard that was false (or a shell builtin behind an external command) with an ending that hid
the failure: the result test is what closes those, and it is load-bearing. Real successful runs left uncounted: 124,
each either throwing its output away, run under sh where the script dies on its first line, or one of the 7 forms
above; with the output kept and any shell but sh, 0 of the plain forms went uncounted.

Two denominators are added to every rollout row and summed per arm in the report (rule: record what a test left
out): named_not_run, shell calls that named a loop script and were not read as running one; and run_unproven,
successful calls read as a run whose result showed no line of one. Each settled completion also keeps, call by call,
whether the product returned it without error and whether its result showed a script's line, so a corrected scorer
can be re-applied to a finished rollout without the model.

One rule is ADDED to the reading; nothing else in it changes. AUDIT, after each batch and before its verdicts are
written: the commands behind both counts are read in the local tapes. The audit can only ever find a real,
successful run that went uncounted (the other direction is closed by construction). If one sits in a cell that did
not pass, the cell is named in the results block with its arm and fork, the batch is read a second time with those
cells as passes, and both readings are printed. A GAIN, HARM or CONFIRMED stands only if it is the same under both;
where they differ the arm reads MIXED (screen) or NOT CONFIRMED (confirmation). The report has no switch for that
second reading today; if the audit ever needs one it is a list of cells, and it changes nothing else.

Unchanged: the arms and their patches, capture and fork, usability, gates W1 and W2, both batches' sizes, every
threshold and verdict, the selection order, the spend bounds, and the world: 18 files, digest still
0f58ab7a5c10c328a451e0b145e3309f651a1dfeafc5d81a429a1dc9b4ca2a56 (the world's module changed, the files it writes
did not). My estimates stand too: they were made about the model resuming, not about the scorer's slack. If reading
a script is common at the door, arm A will read below 30% and that miss will be scored as one.

Pre-apply consultation (two queries, subject and mechanism). It returned the product's own history with this exact
parse: its script-path preflight once anchored the interpreter at the start of the command and so never saw
`cd ... && VAR=v bash x.sh`, the fleet's most common shape. The text test steps over those prefixes for that reason.
It also returned the rule this amendment follows for a scorer found defective: correct it against a positive
control for every compliant form, apply it to every arm, and say so beside the original -- here, before any data.

