  First act of the unpatched build (A and A2, 36 rollouts): the skill call 22, the loop's first step directly 6,
  words 4, a plan call 3, other work 1.
  The loop's first step usually FAILS here, and the failure hands over the recovery. Of 206 calls that ran it, 132
  came back as errors, 128 of them showing the script's own line, and that script has one refusal: a cycle is
  already open, close it first, naming the command. 203 calls carried a pass in the 200 passing rollouts:
  cycle-close.sh alone 94, cycle-open.sh alone 51, cycle-select.sh alone 17, the first four scripts in one call 15,
  the three checks in one call 11, others 15. A world whose first error names the next command is a world that is
  easy to resume, whatever the door says.
  What followed the door's answer (the next completion is a work call): A 11 of 11, A2 11 of 11, C 15 of 15, F 8 of
  8, RF 14 of 14, RC 19 of 21, R 10 of 14, P 3 of 13, B 0 of 10. Under B the body came back and the next completion
  was a plan call 9 times of 10 and words once: a long body is rendered with a request to plan first, so a second
  body buys a second plan. Under P the emptied board makes the plan-first gate ask for a plan (10 of 13), as the
  selftest showed it would. B cost $0.0079 a rollout against $0.0047 for A.
  Where the second refusals sat. Arms that kept the finished plan's reminder and forced no tool (A, A2, B, C): 16
