=== 23:39 UTC 2026-09-20 CORRECTION -- THE VETO DOOR, BATCH 1 RESULTS: two sentences about WHY the offline proof missed the C witness were wrong (the scripted call did carry the argument). No count, rate, verdict or spend figure changes
What was wrong. Item 3 of the results block above says the offline proof missed the C witness "because its
scripted model calls the skill with no argument", and its Next paragraph promises the repair: "a scripted call that
carries an argument as its positive control". Both sentences are wrong, and they are mine. The scripted model's
repeated skill call has carried the argument since the instrument's first commit (43dff3c: skill "cycle", argument
"loop", the call the live model makes; the same at 465b332, the commit batch 1 ran). The control I promised already
existed, so its absence cannot have been the cause, and adding it would have repaired nothing.

What actually happened. The selftest never put the question a batch is READ by to a row the real product wrote. It
checked each witness's ingredients by hand (is the first step in the door's answer, is it the body, was a tool
forced, is the reminder in the fork request, which trace notes were written) and never the pointer flag; and it ran
the usability rule only on hand-written rows, which carried the flag as a sound row "should". So the rule was proven
on rows no build would write, and the rows the builds did write were never shown to the rule. Re-run today with
batch 1's detector put back, the scripted door answers the framed pointer and the flag reads false, exactly as in
the live batch. The rule asks a C row at the door for a flagged pointer that carries the first step, so a C tree's
own selftest row, put to the rule, would have been refused as "witness:C-absent": offline, at no cost, before the
batch. (That last step is read off the rule's text, not re-run: the C patch no longer applies to main.)

How I came to write it. I inferred why the proof had missed the fault from the fact that it had passed, and did not
read the scripted call before publishing the explanation. Found the same evening while building the promised
control: the call I went to add was already there.

What is changed (in this commit, with the instrument).
  The door's witness reads the flag the product now sets on a pointer it builds (ADR-0203), not how the text opens.
  The selftest runs the usability rule on every row the real product writes in the tree under test; reads the
  door's row under every other arm's name, where the rule must refuse it unless that arm is the same build (A and
  its placebo); and asserts the door's flag. Shown to be a control and not a formality: with batch 1's detector put
  back, the baseline tree's selftest fails on exactly the flag check (168 ok, 1 failed); as committed it passes in
  the baseline's, the placebo's and R's trees.
  "A clock the replay can freeze" is withdrawn. A prefix that holds a clock stays REFUSED: a refusal costs one
  capture, and a normaliser is a second reading of the prefix that could hide a real divergence. The comparison
  registered below simply does not roll out a fork that no calibration rollout could read.
  bench/README.md carried the same wrong sentence and is corrected in this commit.

What does not change. No count, rate, verdict or spend figure in the results block. Batch 1 reads NOT DISCRIMINATING
exactly as registered, and the corrected-witness figures printed beside it (C 12 of 16, RC 16 of 16) came from the
recorded door answers, not from the explanation withdrawn here.

=== 23:39 UTC 2026-09-20 REGISTRATION 2 -- THE VETO DOOR, THE SECOND REFUSAL: does keeping a finished plan's "answer now" reminder silent after a refused stop cut how often gpt-5.6-luna fails to resume? (one arm, a placebo, and the baseline alone first)
Question. After a Stop hook refuses a stop taken on a FINISHED plan, the product still ends every request that
follows with its one-line reminder for a finished plan (plan complete: answer the original request now). Arm R keeps
that line silent for exactly that plan, until the plan changes. Does R cut the share of rollouts in which
gpt-5.6-luna (served posture, reasoning effort none) does NOT get back to the loop's own work? This block fixes the
arms, the measure, the two stages and the reading BEFORE any scored call. None has been made since batch 1.

Where the hypothesis comes from, declared. From looking at batch 1's rows after the fact: in the arms that kept the
reminder and forced no tool (A, A2, B, C) a rollout did not resume 16 times of 70; in the arms where the reminder
was gone (R, P, RC, RF), 1 time of 70. That was written down as NOT a reading, for a later registration to test.
This is that registration, and because the pattern was found in batch 1's rows, nothing of batch 1 is reused: no
capture, no tape, no row. Every fork is captured afresh and this batch is read alone, never pooled with that one.

Build under test. main at the commit that carries this block. The product has changed once since batch 1 (ADR-0203,
#579): a pointer for a skill call that carries an argument is no longer treated as a fresh body, so the door's
answer lost a stray follow-these-instructions line. Batch 1's baseline (A and A2 together: 7 of 36 did not resume,
on the older build) is therefore a prediction to compare with, not a control. The control is arm A in this batch.

Instrument. The capture-and-fork instrument of the first registration, the same synthesized world (18 files, digest
0f58ab7a5c10c328a451e0b145e3309f651a1dfeafc5d81a429a1dc9b4ca2a56), the outcome as AMENDMENT 1 left it, and the same
usability rule (build, prefix, wire, witness, outcome; an unusable cell is run once more and no further). Changed
since batch 1, all in the commit that carries this block, hashes in the MANIFEST below:
  the door's witness reads the product's own pointer flag (see the CORRECTION above);
  the selftest puts the usability rule to the rows the real product writes, under every arm's name;
  the permutation test is exact at any number of forks (a convolution over the distinct sums, held against
  one-at-a-time enumeration on 7,180 inputs; listing every assignment stops being practical near 25 forks);
  `arms --arms` builds only the arms named; `--stage` gives each stage its own ledger; `--forks-of` keeps the
  comparison off a fork no calibration rollout could read; `report --mode calibrate|refusal` is the reading below,
  with its rates and thresholds held as exact fractions, and is checked through the command line as well as in
  process (its first version could not print its own rule, which only the command line showed).

Arms. Three builds, each a detached worktree of main plus its patches, run as its own process:
  A    this build.
  A2   the PLACEBO: this build again under another name, read against A like any arm. It can never ship.
  R    bench/veto_door_arms/r.patch, the bytes batch 1 ran (sha256 e73a3300...964e0); it applies to main
       unchanged. Once a turn-end hook has refused a stop on a finished plan, that plan's reminder is silent; it
       speaks again the moment the plan changes. Nothing else about the plan changes. Its witness is its trace
       note plus the reminder's absence from the fork request.
Not in this batch: P also empties the board, so it would test two things at once; C and B no longer apply to main
and C loses any fork whose prefix met a pointer; F is another lever. One hypothesis, one arm, one test.

Measure. A usable rollout that is not a PASS did NOT RESUME (a second refused stop, the cap of 6 live completions,
or a turn that ended by itself). Every rate below is the NOT-RESUMED share: lower is better. Batch 1 showed that
this is the failure this world produces, and that the pass side has no room left to show a gain.

Stage 0, captures. Up to 40 runs of the unpatched build for 32 forks, as before (a run's first delivery only).
Fewer than 24 forks captured is NOT MEASURED there and then: no rollout is run.

Stage 1, CALIBRATION: arm A alone, 2 rollouts a fork, in its own ledger. Read (`--mode calibrate`):
  NOT MEASURED        fewer than 24 complete forks. The instrument is fixed and the batch re-registered.
  NOT DISCRIMINATING  A did not resume on fewer than 1 rollout in 15. No comparison is run, nothing ships, R stays
                      untested, and the next registration has to change the WORLD toward the served door, not the
                      arms: this world then does not produce the failure on this build.
  PROCEED             otherwise.
This gate protects the spend, not the reading: it is set low on purpose. Simulated before any data (below), a world
that misses 12% of the time is stopped here about 1 time in 9, one that misses 2% about 9 times in 10. Calibration
rows NEVER enter the comparison: going on because A's rows looked bad, and then comparing against those same rows,
would build a difference out of the selection.

Stage 2, COMPARISON: fresh rollouts of A, A2 and R, 4 a fork each, on every fork the calibration could read at all
(a fork none of whose calibration rollouts was usable, such as a prefix that holds a clock, is not rolled out again;
usability never looks at what the model did, so neither does this). Arms interleaved inside a fork, the starting arm
moving one along per fork and per repetition, four forks at a time. Denominators, fixed now: 128 rollouts an arm at
32 complete forks (one rollout = 0.8 points), 96 at the floor of 24 (1.0 point); a fork's own share is 0, 25, 50, 75
or 100%. Read (`--mode refusal`), in this order:
  NOT MEASURED        fewer than 24 complete forks.
  NOT DISCRIMINATING  A's not-resumed share IN THE COMPARISON is under 8% (10 of 128 or fewer; 7 of 96 or fewer):
                      "flat" would then mean nothing, because there was nothing to reduce.
  otherwise each arm X is read against A over the complete forks, with m = A's share, x = X's share, and p = the
  exact two-sided sign-flip permutation test over the per-fork differences (the fork is the unit):
    VOID    under 90% of its cells filled on the forks A filled.
    HARM    x - m >= 10 points AND p < 0.05.
    GAIN    x <= m / 2 AND m - x >= 5 points AND p < 0.05.
    FLAT    |m - x| < 5 points.
    MIXED   anything else.
  A GAIN or HARM must keep its size with any ONE fork left out (m and x re-taken on each set of forks-minus-one,
  the p not re-taken), or the arm reads MIXED and the forks it rested on are named.
  NOT MEASURED        also if the placebo reads GAIN or HARM: a verdict from nothing means no verdict here counts.
Every threshold is a function of m as MEASURED in the comparison, never of a predicted baseline. Enumerated with the
instrument's own function over every pair of counts (0..128 misses for A, 0..128 for X, both sides of the p), and the
same at 96: each of 16,641 pairs lands in exactly one branch, none unnamed, and GAIN and FLAT cannot both hold (5
points apart). At m = 11 of 128 GAIN needs x <= 4; at 20 of 128, x <= 10; at 38 of 128, x <= 19; at 64, x <= 32.
The p has its own floor: k forks that all lean one way give 2 / 2^k, so p < 0.05 needs at least 6 forks that carry a
difference. If fewer than 6 do, R cannot read GAIN whatever it does: whatever it then reads is reported as "the p
was out of reach", and says nothing against R.
AMENDMENT 1's AUDIT applies to each stage, before its reading is written: the commands behind both scorer counts are
read in the local tapes. A real, successful run found uncounted in a cell that did not pass is named with its arm
and fork, the stage is read a second time with those cells as passes, and both readings are printed. PROCEED, GAIN
and HARM stand only if they are the same under both; where they differ the calibration reads NOT DISCRIMINATING and
the arm reads MIXED.

What follows from the reading.
  R reads GAIN        R ships.
  R reads MIXED with x < m, or the p was out of reach with x < m
                      ONE more comparison may be run: fresh captures, the same three arms, this same rule, its own
                      dated block (manifest and spend only), read ALONE and never pooled with this one. If that one
                      reads GAIN, R ships; anything else, nothing ships and the bench ends for R. Two such batches
                      can read a false GAIN at most twice as often as one (simulated: at most 1% each).
  anything else       nothing ships from this bench. FLAT or HARM falsifies the LARGE effect the batch-1 pattern
                      suggested (a small one is not ruled out by this design, and is said not to be); VOID, NOT
                      MEASURED and NOT DISCRIMINATING leave R untested and are said to.
"Ships" means what it meant in the first registration: built properly on main afterwards, with no flag and one
behaviour, tests including the deterministic re-spiral test that the fence still ends a veto stall at three, an ADR,
and the next served sample reads whether the rests went. An outcome not named here is never upgraded to "ships".

Secondary, reported and deciding nothing: first act and how often it was words; what followed the door's answer
(the unpatched build went on to a work call 22 times of 22 in batch 1, on the older build: a before and after
across two builds and two batches is NOT a measurement of ADR-0203); second refused stop against cap; the completion
a pass came on; cost and prompt tokens per rollout; how many forks had a finished plan at the fork (10 of 10 in
batch 1; where it is not finished R's tree IS A's, and such forks stay in the reading because they are part of what
this world produces); how many forks were lost to a clock; and A's share in calibration beside A's share in the
comparison (same build, same forks, other rollouts: the size of plain regression to the mean here).

What the numbers will NOT show, kept apart from the rule on purpose. A GAIN for R is what the reminder hypothesis
predicts, and it is also what "any change to the last message of the request" would predict: this bench does not
separate the two. The numbers decide what ships, not why it works. This world does not reproduce the served door
(the unpatched build passed it 22 times of 22 here, the served one 4 of 8), so a GAIN here is about a second refused
stop in THIS world; what it does to the served loop's rests is the next served sample's question. The segment ends
at the first resumed command: whether the loop then keeps running is not measured. One synthesized world, one
model, one effort, the first refused stop of each run only.

Simulated before any data, with the instrument's own rule, forks as uneven as batch 1's (5 of 10 forks held every
miss; a beta with concentration 0.8), 30 complete forks, 300 to 1,000 runs a cell. If A misses 19% and R cuts that to
a tenth, R reads GAIN 91% of the time; if R halves it, 30%; if R does nothing, 0.0% (at most 1% in any cell of any
variant run). If A misses 12%: 55%, 19%, 0.0%. So this design can see a LARGE effect, which is what the pattern in
batch 1 looked like (1 of 70 against 16 of 70); a halving would most likely read MIXED, and is not claimed to be
detectable. A fourth repetition bought about as much power per dollar as eight more forks and costs less, so it
is 4 repetitions on 32 forks.

My estimates, recorded so they can be scored. Captures: 32 forks reached 90%; about 29 complete in calibration. A in
