simulation gave a tenfold cut 91% at a 19% baseline and 55% at 12%; A came in between them, and I had put 52% on the
branch that happened. Spend: said about $3.00, was $2.2729 (captures $0.9634, calibration $0.4598, comparison
$0.8497) against a hard bound of $3.95. The per-rollout guess ($0.0043) was wrong both ways: $0.0066 an attempt in
calibration, $0.0024 in the comparison. The measured difference beside it is the provider's prompt cache: 68% of
calibration's prompt tokens were read from cache, 98% of A's in the comparison and 95% of R's (a fork's first
replays pay for its prefix; later ones mostly do not). Campaign live spend is now approximately $9.59.

Secondary, reported and deciding nothing.
  What a miss was. All 38 misses (A 18, A2 20) were a second refused stop: none was the cap of 6 live completions,
  none a turn that ended by itself. 33 of the 38 began with words and no tool call, and every rollout whose first
  act was words ended as a second refused stop (A 16 of 16, A2 17 of 17). Under R no first act was words: 0 of 116.
  First act (A / A2 / R): the skill again 63 / 63 / 69; the first step 19 / 16 / 18; the plan 14 / 17 / 22; a loop
  script 4 / 3 / 7; words 16 / 17 / 0.
  After the door's answer: A went on to the first step 55 times and to the plan 8, of 63; A2 57, 5 and a script
  once, of 63; R 50 and 19, of 69. In no arm was a door answer followed by a stop.
  The completion a pass came on (1st / 2nd / 3rd / 4th / 5th): A 19 / 46 / 29 / 3 / 1; A2 14 / 53 / 28 / 1 / 0;
  R 21 / 52 / 36 / 7 / 0.
  Cost and size of a rollout: A $0.0022, 86.6k prompt tokens, 2.04 live completions; A2 $0.0021, 85.4k, 2.01;
  R $0.0030, 96.1k, 2.25. R's rollouts are the longer ones because none of them ended early at a stop.
  A in calibration beside A in the comparison (same build, same forks, other rollouts): 19.0% and 15.5%, so plain
  regression to the mean was 3.5 points here, beside a difference of 15.5.
  Batch 1's baseline as a prediction (A and A2, older build: 7 of 36, 19.4%): A and A2 here, 38 of 232, 16.4%.
  Forks lost to a prefix the replay could not reproduce: 3 of 32, 12 attempts, $0.0994.

What the numbers do NOT show, as registered. R's GAIN is what the reminder hypothesis predicts, and it is also what
"any change to the last message of the request" predicts: this bench does not separate the two, and nothing here
says WHY R works. That words-first rollouts are where every miss began, and that R had none, is a description of
these rows, not a tested mechanism. This world is not the served door: the GAIN is about a second refused stop in
this world, on one model at one effort, for the first refused stop of each run, and the segment ends at the first
resumed command. Whether the served loop's rests go is the next served sample's question; whether the loop then
keeps running was not measured. And 0 of 116 is not "never": the Wilson 95% interval for R's not-resumed share runs
to 3.2%.

